 ],
 "literal": "To hostility yielding is prosperity; that to increase if one desires, will hasten indeed ruin.",
 "translation": "Yielding before hostility brings prosperity; but if one strives to triumph in it, ruin will surely hasten upon them.",
 "modern": "பகைமைக்கு பணிந்து போவது ஒருவருக்கு ஆக்கத்தைத் தரும்; அதனை வளர்க்க விரும்பினால், அது அவருக்கு அழிவை விரைந்து கொண்டுவரும்.",
 "explanation": "This couplet contrasts the outcomes of two different responses to hostility (இகல்). Yielding or bending (எதிர்சாய்தல்) when faced with enmity is presented as a wise path that leads to prosperity (ஆக்கம்), much like a reed that survives a storm by bending. On the other hand, if a person actively desires to escalate or triumph (மிகல்) in that hostility, it will surely hasten (ஊக்கும்) their own ruin (கேடு). The poet emphasizes that avoiding conflict is a source of strength, while pursuing it guarantees destruction.",
